{"name":"Night View","hex":"#465560","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"night","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The time when the Sun is below the horizon when the sky is dark.","The period of darkness beginning at the end of evening astronomical twilight when the sun is 18 degrees below the horizon, and ending at the beginning of morning astronomical twilight."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Ellipsis of good night."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/night"},{"term":"view","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Visual perception.\nThe act of seeing or looking at something.","The act of seeing or looking at something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To look at.","To regard in a stated way."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/view"}],"entries":[{"title":"Night vision","blocks":[{"text":"Night vision is the ability to see in low-light conditions, either naturally with scotopic vision or through a night-vision device. Night vision requires both sufficient spectral range and sufficient intensity range. Humans have poor night vision compared to many animals such as cats, dogs, foxes and rabbits, in part because the human eye lacks a tapetum lucidum, tissue behind the retina that reflects light back through the retina thus increasing the light available to the photoreceptors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Nightvision.jpg/330px-Nightvision.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_vision","wikidata":"Q5353651"},{"title":"Night","blocks":[{"text":"Night, or nighttime, is the period of darkness when the Sun is below the horizon. Daylight illuminates one side of Earth, leaving the other in darkness. The opposite of nighttime is daytime. Earth's rotation causes the appearance of sunrise and sunset. Moonlight, airglow, starlight, and light pollution dimly illuminate night. The duration of day, night, and twilight varies depending on the time of year and the observer's latitude."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg/330px-Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night","wikidata":"Q575"},{"title":"Midnight blue","blocks":[{"text":"Midnight blue is a dark shade of blue named for its resemblance to the apparently blue color of a moonlit night sky around a full moon. Midnight blue is identifiably blue to the eye in sunlight or full-spectrum light, but can appear black under certain more limited spectra sometimes found in artificial lighting. It is similar to navy, which is also a dark blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg/330px-D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midnight_blue","wikidata":"Q947638"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Human_eye_in_dim_light.jpg/500px-Human_eye_in_dim_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Human_eye_in_dim_light.jpg/1280px-Human_eye_in_dim_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Human_eye_in_dim_light.jpg/500px-Human_eye_in_dim_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The pupil of the eye dilates in the dark to enhance night vision. Shown here is a pupil of an adult naturally dilated to 9 mm in diameter in mesopic light levels. The average human eye is not able to dilate to this extent without the use of mydriatics.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuman_eye_in_dim_light.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/M3_Sniperscope.jpg/500px-M3_Sniperscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/M3_Sniperscope.jpg/1280px-M3_Sniperscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/M3_Sniperscope.jpg/500px-M3_Sniperscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"USMC M3 Sniperscope assembled on a M3 Carbine. Introduced during the Korean War, it was an early active infrared night vision equipment powered by a large 12 volt battery that was carried in a rubberized canvas backpack.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM3_Sniperscope.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/M60_PattonMediumTank.jpg/500px-M60_PattonMediumTank.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/M60_PattonMediumTank.jpg/1280px-M60_PattonMediumTank.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/M60_PattonMediumTank.jpg/500px-M60_PattonMediumTank.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An M60 tank with an infrared searchlight mounted on the cannon.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM60_PattonMediumTank.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Nachtsichtgeraet.jpg/500px-Nachtsichtgeraet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Nachtsichtgeraet.jpg/500px-Nachtsichtgeraet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Binocular night vision goggles on a flight helmet. Th